 ## When to use this skill
 
 Use this skill when you need to run coding agents through `acpx`, manage persistent ACP sessions, queue prompts, override the Claude system prompt, prune stale sessions, consume structured agent output from scripts, or compose multi-agent workflows declaratively with `acpx/flows`.
+
+## Shell execution policy
+
+Use a named persistent session for every shell task, including a single prompt.
+Create a unique session with `sessions ensure` once, then use explicit `prompt -s`
+for each turn. Follow [the checked launch procedure](../acpx/SKILL.md#launch-an-external-task-in-a-named-session)
+for scope checks, model selection, permissions, receipts, and the Orca view.
+The command examples below omit that plumbing and assume new, unused task names;
+replace sample names with unique ones when starting independent work.
+
+For a supplied name or follow-up, verify the active saved record, exact cwd,
+agent command, record/backend IDs, model, and thinking. Send `prompt -s` directly;
+do not run `ensure` or `new`, or pass model/configuration overrides. Report a
+missing, closed, or mismatched session instead of creating a replacement.
+Keep the session available after completion and report its name to the caller.
+Before continuing a failed, cancelled, or partially completed task, or after a
+timeout, crash, lost terminal, or missing final result, follow the
+[recovery procedure](../acpx/references/harness-lanes.md#recover-an-interrupted-task).
+It covers request correlation, cancellation, partial effects, and expired route
+guards. Do not refresh routing merely to resume an existing task.
+
+Never use `exec` for shell delegation. `exec` and `compare` below are upstream
+API references; both use disposable sessions. To compare agents, give each a
+distinct named session and send the same prompt to each through `prompt`.
 
